WEBVTT

00:00:00.960 --> 00:00:25.600
I can't understand the loneliness you feel, all this hurt and crying, it's also very real, but the day shall come when I'll return to you again, and then we'll live again.

00:00:30.000 --> 00:00:58.590
That day, kisses with you.

00:01:01.116 --> 00:01:04.376
But the day shall come when I'll return.

00:01:05.948 --> 00:01:06.248
Yes.

00:01:08.284 --> 00:01:09.124
And then...

00:01:10.556 --> 00:01:11.196
We'll live again.

00:02:02.940 --> 00:02:04.230
I can't understand.

00:02:04.850 --> 00:02:06.270
Lung in the steel.

00:02:11.240 --> 00:02:14.150
Crying also very real.

00:02:16.880 --> 00:02:25.120
Shall come when I then will begin.

00:02:30.000 --> 00:02:59.980
Thank you.

00:04:00.000 --> 00:04:07.470
And where I keep falling and falling and falling and there's nothing to hold on to.

00:04:08.870 --> 00:04:12.450
Only this time it was you instead of me.

00:04:12.670 --> 00:04:16.310
Only it felt like me. I'm so scared.

00:04:18.470 --> 00:04:23.680
It's all right, darling. I won't fall and you'll always have me to hold on to.

00:04:30.000 --> 00:04:34.060
Oh, I don't know. I couldn't sleep. I don't know why.

00:04:35.080 --> 00:04:36.520
I know why. What time is it?

00:04:38.530 --> 00:04:38.850
Late.

00:04:40.300 --> 00:04:41.080
What time do you leave?

00:04:42.520 --> 00:04:42.780
Early.

00:04:49.080 --> 00:04:52.670
Too soon. Would you like some coffee?

00:04:53.620 --> 00:04:53.820
Nope.

00:04:55.130 --> 00:04:58.870
Hot chocolate? Is there anything you do want?

00:05:05.890 --> 00:05:13.330
I want to go for a swim.

00:06:00.000 --> 00:06:01.800
I didn't think it would be like this.

00:06:01.900 --> 00:06:04.810
I know. I didn't either.

00:06:05.380 --> 00:06:06.500
Oh, but I do love you.

00:06:08.350 --> 00:06:09.810
I've been keeping myself so busy,

00:06:09.810 --> 00:06:12.220
I haven't had time to think of you being gone.

00:06:13.220 --> 00:06:14.340
Until just now.

00:06:14.770 --> 00:06:19.900
There's so little time left.

00:06:21.830 --> 00:06:24.990
Oh, darling, hold me. Hold me.

00:06:27.790 --> 00:06:29.750
It would be so bad if only we could have a baby.

00:06:35.210 --> 00:06:37.290
But you can never tell what might happen up there.

00:06:37.630 --> 00:06:40.960
And I do want to have your child just...

00:06:40.960 --> 00:06:42.040
Something does happen.

00:06:42.830 --> 00:06:43.350
It won't.

00:06:43.690 --> 00:06:45.800
We're going to be fine, darling.

00:06:46.800 --> 00:06:47.060
Just fine.

00:08:06.970 --> 00:08:07.470
Hello, Nick.

00:08:08.230 --> 00:08:08.550
Alice?

00:08:09.190 --> 00:08:12.840
Oh, I was hoping it'd call.

00:08:13.060 --> 00:08:18.630
I guess maybe we are still on the same wavelength, huh?

00:08:20.380 --> 00:08:22.880
I didn't think I could take it seeing you go off on that thing,

00:08:22.960 --> 00:08:25.380
but just can't seem to stay away.

00:08:26.970 --> 00:08:27.830
Hey, I understand.

00:08:28.730 --> 00:08:29.290
Are you all right?

00:08:31.760 --> 00:08:32.320
How about you?

00:08:33.540 --> 00:08:33.980
I'm all right.

00:08:39.140 --> 00:08:40.580
I think I'll be all right when I get back.

00:08:40.900 --> 00:08:41.800
When you get back.

00:08:42.200 --> 00:08:42.840
If you get back.

00:08:43.970 --> 00:08:44.530
I'll get back.

00:08:45.180 --> 00:08:47.520
Nobody can guarantee that, not even Lawson.

00:08:47.850 --> 00:08:48.950
I said I'll get back.

00:08:49.130 --> 00:08:52.530
And you always do exactly what you say you're going to do, don't you?

00:08:52.690 --> 00:08:53.310
Alice, why?

00:08:53.490 --> 00:08:54.010
Why now?

00:08:54.110 --> 00:08:55.570
Now of all times, why, huh?

00:08:55.690 --> 00:08:57.430
Because I can't stand it anymore.

00:08:57.570 --> 00:08:58.150
That's why.

00:08:58.800 --> 00:08:59.040
Honey?

00:09:00.730 --> 00:09:02.930
Everything's gonna be all right after I get back from this job.

00:09:03.500 --> 00:09:03.860
Honest.

00:09:05.260 --> 00:09:06.760
When I get back, that'll be the end of it, okay?

00:09:07.540 --> 00:09:10.720
Oh, Nick, what am I going to do?

00:09:11.990 --> 00:09:13.150
I love you, but...

00:09:13.560 --> 00:09:16.300
But I know there'll never be an end of it.

00:09:17.650 --> 00:09:21.150
There'll always be that one more place where nobody's ever been before.

00:09:22.880 --> 00:09:24.880
One day, won't come back.

00:09:28.160 --> 00:09:29.570
Alice, listen to me.

00:09:30.670 --> 00:09:42.380
this is the last adventure i promise truly nick i promise need you and i'll wait for you but

00:09:43.270 --> 00:09:50.550
please don't be a hero go now wish me luck good luck

00:10:35.280 --> 00:10:38.140
Pacific laser communications and Greenland relays from Fort Greene.

00:10:38.710 --> 00:10:39.690
Pass that on to S-1.

00:10:40.330 --> 00:10:41.590
That would check the Sydney station, too.

00:10:42.220 --> 00:10:44.700
Right. They just started the final CONCOM check.

00:10:45.060 --> 00:10:47.920
Good. I want another check on locks pressure valves three and four.

00:10:48.530 --> 00:10:50.370
See that the medics are ready in 75 minutes?

00:10:50.790 --> 00:10:52.090
By the way, have the boys been picked up yet?

00:10:52.290 --> 00:10:53.950
Not yet, sir. We haven't located Grant.

00:10:56.460 --> 00:10:59.670
Oh, we'll see that they're here in 60 minutes.

00:11:00.060 --> 00:11:01.140
Are you ready for the final check-up?

00:11:01.260 --> 00:11:01.560
Yes, sir.

00:11:30.000 --> 00:11:44.340
Hey, Doug, you all packed?

00:11:45.390 --> 00:11:45.750
Packed.

00:11:45.950 --> 00:11:49.930
The bachelor just locks the front door and pays the rent for a year or so.

00:11:50.870 --> 00:11:51.730
Nervous about today?

00:11:51.990 --> 00:11:52.690
No, not at all.

00:11:52.770 --> 00:11:53.270
I feel great.

00:11:54.140 --> 00:11:54.880
It'd be a bus ride.

00:11:55.040 --> 00:11:55.540
A bus ride.

00:11:55.890 --> 00:11:56.870
Yeah, well, you're the navigator.

00:11:56.870 --> 00:11:57.370
I'm the driver.

00:11:57.650 --> 00:11:58.750
And Nick's the passenger.

00:12:00.060 --> 00:12:02.200
I have two years of training just so I can bring back a few rocks.

00:12:02.680 --> 00:12:04.260
What rocks, my boy? What rocks?

00:12:05.380 --> 00:12:06.640
Where's our geologist, anyway?

00:12:08.240 --> 00:12:09.460
Ready for your examination, boys?

00:12:09.560 --> 00:12:10.080
Yeah, where's Grant?

00:12:10.280 --> 00:12:10.980
He's already started.

00:12:11.080 --> 00:12:11.620
Well, let's go.

00:12:16.260 --> 00:12:16.960
Oh, there you are.

00:12:17.100 --> 00:12:18.200
You finally made it, huh?

00:12:18.220 --> 00:12:20.940
After they finish this physical, I won't even be able to climb up the gantry.

00:12:21.210 --> 00:12:22.890
Well, enjoy it, boys. Your last six hours on Earth.

00:12:23.030 --> 00:12:23.270
All right, Doc.

00:12:23.410 --> 00:12:23.870
For a while.

00:12:24.050 --> 00:12:24.650
Yeah, we hope.

00:12:25.150 --> 00:12:26.470
This will be your job from now on.

00:12:28.580 --> 00:12:29.500
Any news about the Russians?

00:12:30.990 --> 00:12:32.250
That where it would be in the headlines.

00:12:33.160 --> 00:12:33.840
Not necessarily.

00:12:34.320 --> 00:12:37.080
How could they just disappear like that?

00:12:39.110 --> 00:12:40.770
Maybe the Russians are keeping quiet about it.

00:12:42.500 --> 00:12:42.560
Hey.

00:12:43.300 --> 00:12:45.740
There's trouble retaining contact with Con Con 3.

00:12:46.140 --> 00:12:47.600
Try switching to the opening channel.

00:12:47.840 --> 00:12:48.020
Right.

00:12:48.800 --> 00:12:50.490
Well, gentlemen, this is it.

00:12:50.790 --> 00:12:51.350
The day.

00:12:52.310 --> 00:12:52.930
How are they, Doctor?

00:12:53.440 --> 00:12:53.760
Perfect.

00:12:54.630 --> 00:12:55.890
No sign of pre-flight stress?

00:12:56.530 --> 00:12:58.550
Nothing that a year and a half vacation will cure.

00:12:59.290 --> 00:12:59.650
Heh heh.

00:13:00.000 --> 00:13:02.100
Well, you won't exactly be on a vacation up there.

00:13:02.780 --> 00:13:03.740
You'll be quite busy.

00:13:04.560 --> 00:13:07.320
However, we'll be quite busy down here, too.

00:13:09.670 --> 00:13:15.260
However, Nick, remember, don't be over-enthusiastic about picking up too many samples.

00:13:16.400 --> 00:13:19.490
Weight and fuel might be a problem when we return.

00:13:20.250 --> 00:13:23.150
Uh, yes, sir. We'll save the boulders for the next trip.

00:13:24.930 --> 00:13:27.800
I think we have covered everything.

00:13:27.800 --> 00:13:29.980
Tell him to suit up.

00:13:30.670 --> 00:13:31.870
Only a short time before entry.

00:13:32.630 --> 00:13:33.370
Well, let's go.

00:13:43.460 --> 00:13:44.640
Longeroo with your ship.

00:13:45.060 --> 00:13:46.000
Good luck.

00:13:46.540 --> 00:13:47.100
Yeah, thanks.

00:13:47.920 --> 00:13:48.820
Uh, how's the orbit?

00:13:49.340 --> 00:13:49.800
Not yet.

00:13:50.260 --> 00:13:57.210
Of course.

00:13:59.070 --> 00:13:59.970
Longeroo at 4th.

00:14:20.320 --> 00:14:25.650
We are pretty well covered for any of the ordinary emergencies here.

00:14:26.940 --> 00:14:29.460
However, you'll be responsible for the...

00:14:30.860 --> 00:14:39.590
By the extraordinaire, if it should happen, bon voyage, I've seen in about 18 months.

00:14:54.130 --> 00:14:59.200
...Nots Duncan and Grant are now inside the cone-shaped caps resting atop the missile.

00:15:00.000 --> 00:15:04.020
spacecraft towers above the field, waiting the final seconds of the countdown.

00:15:05.020 --> 00:15:08.640
This will mark the beginning of a journey that will last approximately nine months.

00:15:09.080 --> 00:15:11.140
Months, 200s at mission control.

00:15:11.540 --> 00:15:16.480
Millions across the nation will wait patiently for words of man's first steps on Mars.

00:15:17.080 --> 00:15:22.780
In 1958, the United States announced its plans to start a program aimed at manned orbital flight.

00:15:23.380 --> 00:15:28.360
Now having accomplished that feat, man is reaching past the stars, the distant planet, Mars.

00:15:30.000 --> 00:15:32.980
One paramedical team reports everything A-OK.

00:15:33.860 --> 00:15:35.200
Ten minutes and counting.

00:15:36.140 --> 00:15:39.630
All systems go. All systems go.

00:15:55.730 --> 00:15:57.000
Start for a year.

00:16:01.150 --> 00:16:12.910
Radio list reading loud and clear, four minutes and counting, attempt ALK, three minutes and

00:16:12.910 --> 00:16:13.210
counting.

00:16:14.440 --> 00:16:23.570
CK to M1, M1 is communications clear, communications systems are good, YTO set, roger, YTO set,

00:16:24.200 --> 00:16:26.800
all astronauts secure, checked.

00:16:30.600 --> 00:16:32.040
Two minutes and counting.

00:16:32.700 --> 00:16:35.000
All personnel will please clear the field.

00:16:53.430 --> 00:16:54.890
Systems go. Good luck.

00:17:00.000 --> 00:17:00.580
and counting.

00:19:02.780 --> 00:19:03.060
Roger.

00:19:27.570 --> 00:19:28.210
Looking good from here.

00:19:33.610 --> 00:19:34.430
Give us cabin pressure.

00:19:35.090 --> 00:19:36.790
Cabin won.

00:21:43.244 --> 00:21:48.244
CK from M1, we read you five by five, CK from N1, we read you five by five.

00:21:51.308 --> 00:21:53.428
Pressure's leveling off at 6.8.

00:21:53.868 --> 00:21:54.968
22 amps.

00:21:55.916 --> 00:21:57.916
The power is still good.

00:21:58.796 --> 00:21:59.976
There goes Biko on time.

00:22:00.000 --> 00:22:02.710
Roger. Understand. Bico.

00:22:04.370 --> 00:22:06.380
Pitch is 37.

00:22:08.520 --> 00:22:13.450
Roger. Pitch is 3-7. Repeat. 3-7.

00:22:14.070 --> 00:22:15.510
One CPS way in yaw.

00:22:28.610 --> 00:22:29.830
Repeat. Zero.

00:22:30.828 --> 00:22:31.028
Mark.

00:22:31.276 --> 00:22:31.876
Six-zero.

00:23:03.740 --> 00:23:04.660
M1 to CK.

00:23:05.440 --> 00:23:06.640
You felt booster jettisoned.

00:23:06.680 --> 00:23:07.140
Do you confirm?

00:23:07.900 --> 00:23:08.760
CK to M1.

00:23:08.880 --> 00:23:09.320
We confirm.

00:23:21.620 --> 00:23:22.340
Booster check.

00:23:23.570 --> 00:23:24.590
Booster falling away.

00:23:24.730 --> 00:23:26.010
Port side 15 degrees.

00:23:35.788 --> 00:23:36.728
The light is green.

00:23:37.900 --> 00:23:38.760
Be good. Check complete.

00:23:41.996 --> 00:23:42.196
Roger.

00:23:43.916 --> 00:23:44.276
That's it.

00:23:52.684 --> 00:23:53.684
We read your clip.

00:23:56.492 --> 00:23:57.332
All right, gentlemen, that's it.

00:23:58.156 --> 00:23:59.936
Now, the next phase is to connect up with our supply ship.

00:24:00.000 --> 00:24:00.840
in about five hours.

00:24:01.000 --> 00:24:01.780
We'll set the watches now.

00:24:01.900 --> 00:24:02.500
Dunk, you take first.

00:24:03.080 --> 00:24:03.480
Roger.

00:24:03.740 --> 00:24:04.820
Wake me in four hours.

00:24:06.020 --> 00:24:07.320
Boy, could I use some shut-eye.

00:24:07.600 --> 00:24:09.280
I hardly had any sleep at all last night.

00:24:10.740 --> 00:24:11.680
No kidding, Mike.

00:24:30.480 --> 00:24:32.680
A workplace where nobody has ever been before.

00:24:34.500 --> 00:24:35.940
One day you just won't come home.

00:24:37.220 --> 00:24:38.910
Alice, listen to me.

00:24:39.920 --> 00:24:45.660
This is the last I'd be a hero.

00:25:04.160 --> 00:25:09.280
Fifth hour of flight. Adjusted atmospheric pressure and space module, operating smoothly.

00:25:10.240 --> 00:25:14.180
Able to walk about freely and in comfort. Weightlessness, no problem.

00:25:14.960 --> 00:25:17.140
Controlled temperature comfortable at 68 degrees.

00:25:18.120 --> 00:25:21.300
Slight roll and vibration, not interfering with normal movement.

00:25:22.900 --> 00:25:25.860
And we are about to experience the first meal of our flight.

00:25:31.530 --> 00:25:32.870
Hey Nick, wake up Mike, will you?

00:25:33.920 --> 00:25:35.540
I don't know, he looks pretty peaceful to me.

00:25:35.940 --> 00:25:37.140
I wouldn't want him to miss my red shirt.

00:25:39.720 --> 00:25:46.740
I'm away, of course.

00:25:53.390 --> 00:25:54.250
Turn down there, red or green?

00:25:54.900 --> 00:25:55.060
Yellow.

00:26:18.040 --> 00:26:18.340
Nick?

00:26:26.000 --> 00:26:27.400
Tell me a sandwich. What does it look like?

00:26:28.310 --> 00:26:29.030
Where'd you get past?

00:26:30.350 --> 00:26:31.850
Quite a delicate fashion, we're off.

00:26:55.100 --> 00:26:58.780
Nice to meet you.

00:26:58.780 --> 00:26:59.980
17,200 miles.

00:27:00.000 --> 00:27:01.200
Alitude?

00:27:02.400 --> 00:27:06.240
Apogee 600 miles, perigee 475.

00:27:06.660 --> 00:27:07.420
What level point?

00:27:08.230 --> 00:27:12.720
43.20 west, 22.50 south.

00:27:13.610 --> 00:27:15.950
Incidentally, according to my calculations, it'll be over Brazil.

00:27:16.390 --> 00:27:16.610
Really?

00:27:17.420 --> 00:27:18.040
All right.

00:27:18.330 --> 00:27:24.880
We have exactly 32 minutes and 28 seconds in which to complete the link-up, restart engines

00:27:24.880 --> 00:27:26.020
and get on course again.

00:27:27.020 --> 00:27:29.970
In precisely eight minutes, we begin link-up.

00:27:30.030 --> 00:27:30.330
CJ.

00:28:00.090 --> 00:28:02.390
M1 from CK. M1 from CK.

00:28:02.830 --> 00:28:04.870
This is M1. Go ahead, CK.

00:28:05.400 --> 00:28:07.140
Supplier ship should be in sight.

00:28:07.340 --> 00:28:08.300
Two degrees to port.

00:28:08.820 --> 00:28:11.860
Approximately 1,000 yards ahead and slightly above you.

00:28:12.180 --> 00:28:12.660
Confirmed.

00:28:13.280 --> 00:28:14.000
We have it in sight.

00:28:20.060 --> 00:28:21.040
On the button, Cliff.

00:28:26.780 --> 00:28:27.960
Proceeding with docking maneuver.

00:28:46.380 --> 00:28:49.140
Zero, nine point four degrees and holding steady.

00:28:49.400 --> 00:28:51.060
Standby with three seconds boosted acceleration.

00:28:51.340 --> 00:28:52.180
In 10 seconds.

00:28:55.110 --> 00:28:56.160
Three, two.

00:29:02.460 --> 00:29:12.550
fast, but off the port. Correction. Port. 103 yards and increasing. Ten by. Port yaw thrust in.

00:29:12.950 --> 00:29:24.880
Mark. Beautiful, beautiful. Dead center. 100 yards. Dead ahead and closing.

00:29:25.480 --> 00:29:28.410
30 yards and slowing. We're coming at a few feet high.

00:29:30.000 --> 00:29:31.340
Stand by.

00:29:31.660 --> 00:29:41.000
Here it comes.

00:30:01.630 --> 00:30:13.500
Maneuver completed, link up accomplished successfully, starting rocket motors to continue flight, over and out.

00:30:14.720 --> 00:30:18.330
Next step, 35 million miles.

00:31:03.580 --> 00:31:05.860
Oh, no, thank you. I'd grab the sandwich inside.

00:31:08.830 --> 00:31:11.330
So tell me, what's the latest news from the rover boys?

00:31:11.530 --> 00:31:12.510
Are they still roving?

00:31:13.610 --> 00:31:14.330
Still roving.

00:31:14.900 --> 00:31:15.920
I read my own target.

00:31:16.780 --> 00:31:18.560
They made a perfect course correction yesterday.

00:31:19.500 --> 00:31:22.820
According to our computers, well on their way to Mars.

00:31:23.420 --> 00:31:25.420
Well, let me know when they're well on their way home.

00:31:25.640 --> 00:31:27.020
I will.

00:31:30.220 --> 00:31:32.880
This is the oddest concoction I've ever seen in my life.

00:31:33.500 --> 00:31:37.200
Garlic pickles, chocolate, ice cream, mangoes, and...

00:31:37.450 --> 00:31:38.650
It looks revolting, is it?

00:31:38.930 --> 00:31:39.490
It is.

00:31:40.950 --> 00:31:43.950
Where did you find the formula? Under a rock?

00:31:44.700 --> 00:31:46.660
Something I inherited from my mother.

00:31:47.400 --> 00:31:48.860
I don't understand. I don't get it.

00:31:49.460 --> 00:31:50.600
Well, you're not supposed to.

00:31:51.000 --> 00:31:51.860
Mike is.

00:31:52.040 --> 00:31:52.400
Lost?

00:31:55.700 --> 00:31:58.050
Well, all right, as long as you're both well and happy.

00:31:58.710 --> 00:31:59.830
Yes, but, uh...

00:32:01.080 --> 00:32:04.830
They're fine. Their biggest problem is to keep in shame.

00:32:05.010 --> 00:32:07.210
That should be no problem for Mike.

00:32:07.610 --> 00:32:10.590
And Nick's been climbing mountains all his life, one after the other.

00:32:11.250 --> 00:32:12.970
Why, I sure hope this is the last one.

00:32:13.410 --> 00:32:15.980
Metaphorically, I don't. He'll be too valuable.

00:32:16.640 --> 00:32:17.300
For what?

00:32:17.950 --> 00:32:21.280
Future Voyagers. He can be direct or...

00:32:21.280 --> 00:32:24.080
No. No, absolutely not. I want him here.

00:32:24.830 --> 00:32:27.250
You go find yourself another scientist to play with.

00:32:27.500 --> 00:32:28.820
I want Nick home.

00:32:31.020 --> 00:32:34.760
Wait a minute, Cliff, nothing can go wrong, can it?

00:32:35.600 --> 00:32:40.250
I mean, nothing is going to go wrong, is it?

00:32:40.780 --> 00:32:42.600
No, of course not.

00:33:00.220 --> 00:33:04.600
Now, according to instructions, we begin today, a series of in-mission re-briefing sessions.

00:33:04.760 --> 00:33:05.420
Nick, you first.

00:33:06.020 --> 00:33:13.180
Item. Mars is 35,112,500 miles from Earth at an average speed of 63,000 miles per hour,

00:33:13.200 --> 00:33:14.420
and the trip should last...

00:33:15.250 --> 00:33:19.090
The trip should last 230 days, 5 hours, and 15 minutes.

